WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.920
These are the scars of Monkey D. Luffy,&nbsp;
the hero of One Piece. The trademark one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.920
he has under his left eye, that he inflicts on&nbsp;
himself to prove his determination to become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:18.480
pirate to Shanks. And of course the large gruesome&nbsp;
looking one on his chest in the same of a giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:25.280
X where Akainu's attack hit him after he had&nbsp;
taken Ace's life. Both are reminders of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:32.720
two most life-altering moments in Luffy's life, a&nbsp;
visual representation of Luffy's determination to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:39.040
become a great pirate and of the tragic loss of&nbsp;
his older brother and his failure at Marineford.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:45.120
And when I thought about it for a second, it hit&nbsp;
me. It's not only Luffy's scars that represent who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:52.240
he is as a person and what path he follows,&nbsp;
no, this is true for every single character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:59.360
in the story that carries a scar. Zoro, Kaido,&nbsp;
Shanks, Nami, Shanks, Katakuri, Whitebeard. Every,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:05.920
single scar they carry is a manifestation of what&nbsp;
they have gone through and what they stand for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:12.080
But it gets even crazier than that, because I&nbsp;
think I have discovered that these scars reveal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.560
to us many of the things that still have&nbsp;
to happen before the end of the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:23.520
making it one of the most badass ways of&nbsp;
storytelling in the entire story to me.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.880
And for me, Luffy is of course the best&nbsp;
person to begin with, because, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:35.120
he's the protagonist. So what do the marks on&nbsp;
his rubbery body tell us about the end of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:41.680
piece? Well, as I've said, these scars represent&nbsp;
the most important events in Luffy's entire life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:47.680
both of which are still unresolved. The knife&nbsp;
cut under his left eye is a constant reminder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:54.000
throughout the story, that our rubbery boy had&nbsp;
one dream and one dream only his entire life:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.640
Not becoming a doctor and then a marine and&nbsp;
then a pirate. No. Luffy wanted to become the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:07.120
pirate king ever since he met shanks. To me it&nbsp;
almost feels like a physical representation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:12.880
his oath that he will become a great pirate.&nbsp;
It's also a reminder of Shanks' sacrifice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.120
the lesson he taught luffy in the pub&nbsp;
with the mountain bandits and of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:25.280
the moment Luffy consumed his devil fruit, that&nbsp;
set him on his path to become a pirate captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.560
and start his own crew. It also proves to us&nbsp;
and I guess to Luffy himself as well right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:37.200
from the start, that he is aware that the path to&nbsp;
freedom will be painful and full of sacrifices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.360
but that he is willing to take all of it on.&nbsp;
And so I think it's pretty fair to say that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:50.320
little scar, is a summary of Luffy's origins&nbsp;
and his motivation for what he is doing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:56.800
trying to achieve throughout the story.&nbsp;
But what does it tell us about the ending?

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:02.320
Well as you probably have guessed, this scar&nbsp;
also commemorates his promise to Shanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:08.080
return the legendary straw hat that was&nbsp;
passed down from Roger to him to Luffy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:14.640
symbolising Shanks' trust in Luffy&nbsp;
as the one who can change the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.680
While the scar keeps reminding us that Luffy&nbsp;
is still trying to become that great pirate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:25.840
it also promises us a reunion of the two men,&nbsp;
that will mark Luffy as ready to claim the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:32.800
throne of the pirate world. Which as you know, I&nbsp;
believe should be happening pretty soon now. I see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:39.040
this scar basically as a guarantee that we will&nbsp;
have a reunion between Shanks and luffy and of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:45.280
Luffy becoming pirate king, so that the promise&nbsp;
that is sealed in his cheek can be fulfilled.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:51.680
Shanks, who himself of course carries two major&nbsp;
scars, his lost arm, though I guess that's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:58.160
pretty damn big scar, is basically Shanks'&nbsp;
counterpart to Luffy's reminding us and him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:03.760
of their promise. Meanwhile the three distinct&nbsp;
claw marks over his eyes, that he received during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:10.160
a confrontation with Blackbeard, represent his&nbsp;
futile quest to stop Teach from gaining power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:16.240
something that he has failed to do so far.&nbsp;
This scar also is a promise to us, that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:22.240
relationship between Shanks and Blackbeard will&nbsp;
have a payoff later in the story. It could of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:28.000
course be an actual meeting between the two,&nbsp;
with a chance for Shanks to fulfil his mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.400
though we both know that that would&nbsp;
probably have no good ending for him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:39.760
Or alternatively we will at least get the story&nbsp;
behind Shanks' chase of Teach, and the secrets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:45.840
both of them know about the world, that we are&nbsp;
still unaware of. And so I like to see this triple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:53.120
scar as a representation of Shanks' all-seeing&nbsp;
nature and of his attempt to stop Blackbeard.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:59.520
He even presents it to whitebeard during their&nbsp;
meeting, who by the way mentions his owns scars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.040
he received from Roger when he sees Shanks. In&nbsp;
case you're wondering that's of course because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:11.600
Shanks was part of Rogers crew. So seeing Shanks&nbsp;
reminds Whitebeard of his old rival and all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:18.240
fights and feasts they experienced together.&nbsp;
His major battles and life events commemorated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:24.640
on his chest. They also represent Whitebeards&nbsp;
character, someone who always stood protectingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:30.720
in front of his crew, which he called his family.&nbsp;
Something that Eiichiro Oda, the author of One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:38.000
PIece actually dedicated an entire page to at his&nbsp;
passing, showing a very emotional me at that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:44.960
that this man didn't have a single scar on his&nbsp;
back, never running away and always facing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:52.240
enemies head on. Without any doubt one of my&nbsp;
all-time favorite moments in the entire story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.800
And it's made extra powerful because&nbsp;
Oda-sensei doesn't tell us all this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:01.840
but simply shows us his scars and his&nbsp;
lack thereof on his back. Brilliant.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.560
And of course Whitebeard's death at&nbsp;
Marineford, how he came prepared to die&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:13.120
so that his son Ace could live and how&nbsp;
Akainu put an end to his dream and era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:21.680
all of it is deeply engraved in Luffy's chest in&nbsp;
the shape of his gaping x shaped scar. He received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:27.920
it when his own brother gave his life to protect&nbsp;
his and when Jinbe also risked being killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:33.120
to protect Luffy. As I've discussed in a lot of&nbsp;
detail before in a number character analyses I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:40.240
did on Luffy, Marineford and Ace's death were&nbsp;
the lowest point in his arc and the moment he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:46.320
grew most as a character. The need to take the&nbsp;
world more seriously, to learn that failure is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:54.000
possibility and to get strong enough to protect&nbsp;
his crew and change the world. It's another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.880
promise that Luffy makes to himself when he&nbsp;
starts his training with Rayleigh, that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:04.800
enclosed in his healing wound on his chest. The&nbsp;
X by the way I like to think is a nice nod to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.160
the X that Ace had in his tattoo, in memory&nbsp;
of their third brother Sabo's Jolly Roger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:18.160
The scar also tells us more about Luffy and his&nbsp;
ability to make people love and trust in him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:25.280
because it is the product of his brother and Jinbe&nbsp;
and the whitebeard pirates as a whole protecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:31.480
him. And once again, I think it tells us things&nbsp;
that will definitely happen in the story. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:36.800
is of course Luffy's eventual encounter with&nbsp;
Akainu and how Luffy will deal with the man that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:42.880
killed his brother. Will he fight him or forgive&nbsp;
him. I really cant wait. I personally also like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:49.440
to think that the scar sets up the possible death&nbsp;
of another person close to Luffy, only that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:56.800
time Luffy will be able to stop it. Whether this&nbsp;
is Sabo, one of the straw hats or someone else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:03.280
I don't know, but I feel that this scar will serve&nbsp;
as a reminder of Luffy that he became stronger to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:10.720
not lose another person like he did Ace. If you&nbsp;
wanted to be really poetic you could even say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:15.440
that these scars represent the inherited&nbsp;
will of the people they are connected to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:22.560
Shanks and Ace, that Luffy now carries. And&nbsp;
here I thought scars couldn't get any cooler.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:29.360
But here is the real reason why I think that&nbsp;
these scars let us know exactly what still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:34.800
has to happen in the story. What I've noticed&nbsp;
is that certain fights leave characters like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:41.920
Luffy or Zoro with lasting scars, while others&nbsp;
don't. For example, Luffy doesn't have a scar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:47.760
from the fight with Katakuri, despite probably&nbsp;
being the most vile wound he received yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:54.880
Or being impaled by crocodile. In theory, if&nbsp;
Luffy's little knife left a scar, shouldn't he be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.880 --> 00:09:02.400
covered scars from all his fights? I think the&nbsp;
reason that only certain wounds leave scars is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:09.200
because they represent an unresolved or ongoing&nbsp;
conflict of the character. Luffy needs to meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:15.200
shanks and face akainu. Shanks and blackbeard.&nbsp;
Zoro and Mihawk, though I do want to save Zoro's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:20.320
scars for a whole separate video if you want to&nbsp;
see that. You have the scars of both Akainu and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:26.800
Aokiji reminding them of their unresolved conflict&nbsp;
of how the marines should be lead. But maybe my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:34.240
favourite example of all is the scar that Oden&nbsp;
gave to Kaido. The giant cut across his chest.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:39.120
Because even though Oden is dead,&nbsp;
Kaido's conflict about that fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:46.000
is not over. The fact that he won this duel in an&nbsp;
inhonorable way because someone distracted Oden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:51.680
still haunts him to this day. I go into much more&nbsp;
detail about this in Kaido's character analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:58.880
but to sum it up, this scar embodies the burden&nbsp;
that Kaido has put on himself to reach his goal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:02.800
change the world, even if it&nbsp;
means going against his honor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:09.040
that is so important to him and the joy&nbsp;
of fighting against a strong opponent in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:16.800
fair fight. The scar is there, because Kaido&nbsp;
is still struggling with what it represents.

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:22.880
Nami's scar across her shoulder embodies her&nbsp;
struggle under Arlong and how she got saved by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:29.360
Luffy. The reason you don't see her scar,&nbsp;
is because she decided to tattoo over it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:35.040
making it vanish because the conflict&nbsp;
connected to is has been resolved.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:40.400
Or take brullee and her scar across the&nbsp;
face, that represented her ongoing conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:46.080
with her own family and also Katakuri's&nbsp;
struggle to be the strong older brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:50.720
that can't show any kind of weakness.&nbsp;
As you might remember Brullee got the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:56.400
scar after being attacked by other kids, until&nbsp;
Katakuri stepped in to protect her. Katakrui by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.960
the way with his own scars around his mouth&nbsp;
that he got from eating too many doughnuts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:08.560
that he got bullied for and thus made him&nbsp;
forever hide his face until Luffy came around.

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:16.320
And so the reason, why Luffy DOESN'T carry a scar&nbsp;
from this fight with Katakuri is because there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:22.000
is no more conflict between them. They are&nbsp;
two men who helped each other and found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:29.120
deep respect for their opponent. Katakuri on the&nbsp;
other hand, I wouldn't be at all surprised if he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:35.920
kept a scar of where he stabbed himself. After all&nbsp;
it is a reminder of how luffy changed his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:39.920
physical proof for his honor as&nbsp;
a man for making the fight fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:45.440
and opening up the chance to reunite with&nbsp;
luffy in the future as a possible ally.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:52.240
Another great example for this would be Kid and&nbsp;
his new scar that he got from Shanks. It also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:57.600
embodies the unresolved conflict between them&nbsp;
and how Kid wants to take down the Yonko.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:03.200
And if you want to know the whole story&nbsp;
behind kid and all the rage and anger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:07.120
he has and why he thinks he needs to&nbsp;
take down all the yonko on his own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:13.760
well then you just really can't miss out on this&nbsp;
video here where I explain all that and more.

